     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "i-02",
   "type": "image",
   "props": {
    "url": "/projects/fleur-de-lys-procedural-lily-houdini-unreal/02-step1-hda-in-houdini.webp",
    "caption": "Figure 2. Step 1, creating the HDA. The lily as a Houdini Digital Asset: three folders of parameters on the right, five variations of it on the left.",
    "alt": "Houdini screenshot: five pink lilies in the viewport and the HDA parameters (stem height, stem bend, leaf count, tepal length, seed) on the right.",
    "width": 2560,
    "height": 1496
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "The lily is a Houdini Digital Asset, ",
     "styles": {}
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "zenray_lily",
     "styles": {
      "code": true
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": ", saved in its own .hda file and instanced in the scene. Everything a shot needs is a parameter of this asset or a table inside it, which is what makes the variations of step 7 cheap.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "props": {
    "level": 3
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "Seven components, three folders",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "Inside, seven Python SOPs build the seven families of parts of a lily: stem, leaves, pedicels and buds, tepals, stamens, pistil and calyx. They are merged, given normals, and sent to one output. The final instance has 7,416 primitives and 9,888 points.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "The interface is three folders. ",
     "styles": {}
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "HDA Shape",
     "styles": {
      "bold": true
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": " has eight parameters: stem height, stem bend, leaf count, leaf length, tepal length, tepal flare, pedicel length and seed. ",
     "styles": {}
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "HDA Detail",
     "styles": {
      "bold": true
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": " has eight more: leaf droop, leaf half width, tepal half width, tepal edge wave, stamen length, stem radius, calyx size and funnel. ",
     "styles": {}
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "HDA Layout",
     "styles": {
      "bold": true
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": " holds the flower table, the band table of the trim sheet, a UV mode and two leaf settings. Eight is a limit I set on purpose: a parameter that I never need to touch stays inside the network.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "props": {
    "level": 3
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "Measure first, then model",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "Before shaping anything, I gave the agent a four-view reference sheet (front, side, top and three-quarter) and it was turned into masks. The HDA was then fitted to those masks. The overlap between my silhouettes and the masks started at 0.725 and reached 0.80 on average: 0.76 from the front, 0.68 from the side and 0.78 from the top. The side view is the weakest, and the buds, which the front and side views barely show, are placed by interpretation.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "The place of every flower lives in a table inside the HDA. Each line is a kind (F for a flower, B for a bud), a position along the stem from 0 to 1, an azimuth, a tilt above the horizontal and a size. Moving a flower means editing a number, not selecting an object.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "codeBlock",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "# kind  position  azimuth  tilt  size\nF 1.000 242.0 53.0 0.94\nF 0.861 190.0 22.7 1.13\nF 0.850  11.0 39.0 0.91\nF 0.713 137.0 22.0 1.01\nB 0.700  76.0 15.7 1.00\nB 0.600 325.0 29.0 0.95",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "i-03",
   "type": "image",
   "props": {
    "url": "/projects/fleur-de-lys-procedural-lily-houdini-unreal/03-hda-versions.webp",
    "caption": "Figure 3. The HDA at versions 1, 4 and 7, seen from the front, the side, the top and in three quarters.",
    "alt": "Three rows of four viewport views of the lily model, becoming more detailed from top to bottom.",
    "width": 2400,
    "height": 1820
   }
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "In version 1 the parts exist but the flowers sit low on a stem that is mostly leaves. Version 4 spreads the flowers around the stem. Version 7 is the same model seen closer, with more detail in the tubes, the stamens and the buds. The colours in these previews are flat colours per part; the real ones come later from the trim sheet.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "props": {
    "level": 3
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "UVs made for a trim sheet",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
