  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "On Apple Silicon, Karma XPU runs on the CPU only, through Embree, and its GPU path needs an NVIDIA card. A Karma CPU frame at 1080 px took about 50 seconds. I wanted my M5 Max GPU, so I looked for a free renderer for Solaris on macOS. Cycles, from Blender, has an official Hydra delegate with a Metal backend. It was compiled for Houdini 22.0.368 and rendered the 72 frames of an orbit in about 50 seconds. Octane Prime for Mac installed and loaded, but it needs an OTOY account, and I did not test a render.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "Cycles came out paler than Karma, with a mean saturation of 0.22 against 0.32, because it reads the preview material and not Karma's: no translucency, no sheen, and a normal map read the wrong way that drew black fibres on the petals. A grade after the render (saturation times 1.45, value times 0.93) brought it to 0.34. The white viewport that I had for a while had a surprising cause: the display flag sat on the node that holds the Karma render settings, and with that flag on it Cycles draws solid white. Moving the flag to the light node before it fixed the viewport. The values of the Cycles look development live in a Markdown file that I can edit, and a script applies them to the scene.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "props": {},
   "content": [
    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "None of this reaches the final shots. The transparency still did not look right in Cycles, so I moved the lily into Unreal and rendered with Lumen.",
     "styles": {}
    }
   ]
  },
